## Contract ownership notes
- index.ts is unchanged: it remains the E3.1 barrel only. E3.2+ modules import concrete
  files directly (request-id.ts). E3.3/E3.4 must keep doing the same.
- fingerprintAccount stays in src/lib/observability/redact.ts with signature
  `fingerprintAccount(username: string): string` (HMAC-SHA256, AUTH_SECRET/NEXTAUTH_SECRET,
  trimmed+lowercased username, first 16 hex chars). E3.6 may import it directly; do not
  move or change the signature.
- Redaction is the only behavioural change from the Lead review: DB host:port / Prisma
  connection wording is now always redacted. Event names, fields, schema version and the
  accepted event whitelist are unchanged.

## Residual risks / boundaries (not claimed as complete)
1. Proxy cannot observe the final status of pass-through responses (route/page renders).
   E3.2 therefore logs http.request only for branches the proxy itself produces
   (redirect/401/403 JSON) and the instrumented public route (/api/health). Page-route HTTP
   logging/metrics over all routes is a follow-up for E3.4/E3.3, not a silent pass.
2. /api/auth/*, /api/miniprogram/*, /uploads/* and static assets are intentionally outside
   the matcher; they carry no x-request-id (Auth.js callback 302 verified as unchanged).
3. Route names in proxy logs collapse UUID/numeric/hex segments to :id because the matched
   Next route template is not available in proxy. /api/health uses its exact template.
4. `next dev` rewrites flower-wms-system/tsconfig.json and next-env.d.ts; both were restored
   to the dev baseline after evidence runs (no generated-file diff left by E3.2).
5. Native PostgreSQL 5432 and the dev container 55432 were never touched; all DB evidence
   used throw-away clusters or closed loopback ports.
